acer-powersave for Debian
=========================

Clash with other power managers
-------------------------------
Do not install TLP, tuned or auto-cpufreq alongside this: they all fight over
the same settings, and power-profiles-daemon lists them in Conflicts. If TLP
was ever installed, check it did not leave PPD masked:

    systemctl is-enabled power-profiles-daemon.service   # must not say masked

Clash with PowerDevil "Run a script"
------------------------------------
If you had PowerDevil's "Run a script" action set up to poke the CPU (say
"sudo cpupower frequency-set ..."), remove it: it fights both PPD and this
service, and the winner is whoever writes last. Look in ~/.config/powerdevilrc,
sections [AC][RunScript] and [Battery][RunScript].

Why the CPU frequency is put back
---------------------------------
In power-saver, PPD pins scaling_max_freq to the hardware minimum on top of
setting the EPP. On this hardware that backfires: measured, the same task takes
3.25x longer and burns 3.3x more energy, because the CPU cannot race to idle.
This service undoes the cap a couple of seconds after each profile change.

That delay is not laziness. PPD emits its PropertiesChanged signal and keeps
writing afterwards, so checking immediately finds nothing to undo.

PPD cannot be configured out of this. It has no setting for it; --block-driver
intel_pstate does stop it, but that removes the CPU driver altogether and the
performance profile disappears with it; and platform_profile, the canonical way
to let firmware handle profiles, is rejected by this laptop's firmware. Set
unpin_cpu_freq = no in /etc/acer-powersave/profiles.conf if you disagree.

Session service
---------------
Only whoever owns the graphical session can change the refresh rate, so that
part lives in a separate user service:

    systemctl --user enable --now acer-powersave-session.service

Kernel command line
-------------------
The package installs /etc/default/grub.d/50-acer-powersave.cfg, which adjusts
the kernel command line without touching your /etc/default/grub:

 * drops intel_pstate=passive, which pins the governor to the minimum frequency
   even under load and leaves the EPP inoperative;
 * drops pci_aspm=force, which is not a valid parameter (the real one is
   pcie_aspm);
 * adds pcie_aspm=force, i915.enable_fbc=1 and i915.enable_dc=2 if missing.

Removing the package reverts this and runs update-grub again.

NVIDIA GPU
----------
"acer-powersave gpu off" unloads the modules, removes the card from the PCI bus
and lets the bridge drop to D3cold; "gpu on" brings it back. No reboot and no
envycontrol needed.

By default the card is kept out of the bus from boot: the blacklist in
/etc/modprobe.d/zz-nvidia-debian.conf stops the driver loading, and
acer-nvidia-arranque.service removes the four PCI functions before the display
manager starts. That saves about 500 mW. HDMI needs the card, so run
"gpu on" before plugging a monitor in.

If envycontrol left its udev rule behind, the card is ejected the moment it
appears -- including when "gpu on" rescans the bus:

    sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/50-remove-nvidia.rules
    sudo udevadm control --reload

And if /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-nvidia.conf contains "alias nvidia off" lines,
drop them: they prevent loading the driver even by hand.

Always check the state through sysfs. "lspci" and "nvidia-smi" WAKE the bridge
and give you a false reading:

    cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:01.0/power_state    # D3cold = powered down

